      "document": {
        "id": "4a039675-0cec-4228-9dcb-e82df31dbe92",
        "title": "Mark Zuckerberg gets it right:\n\n",
        "status": "draft",
        "nodes": [
          {
            "id": "2082226325621035131",
            "relation": "root",
            "depth": 0,
            "sourceTweetId": "2082226325621035131",
            "author": {
              "name": "David Sacks",
              "handle": "DavidSacks",
              "avatarUrl": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1879600809693917185/GkBxdTd9_normal.jpg",
              "isVerified": false
            },
            "content": "Mark Zuckerberg gets it right:\n\n“The defining question of our age isn’t whether superintelligence will exist, but who will have access to it. Will it be centralized and restricted to a few institutions, or will it be a tool that empowers everyone?”\n\nConcentration of power is the biggest risk of AI. When a small number of labs (working hand-in-glove with the administrative state) decide who has access to which model capabilities, they inevitably shape what can be said, known, and built. That’s not “safety.” It’s control.\n\nAs Mark points out, the history of open source shows that broad access and transparency are usually the best path to actual security and resilience. Decentralization creates checks and balances on power. By contrast, centralized alternatives, like bureaucratic approval regimes and mandatory gatekeeping, typically produce regulatory capture and reinforce cartels.\n\nPersonal superintelligence in everyone’s hands, with competing models and real data sovereignty, is a far better check on a dystopian future than self-appointed guardians who claim to be “aligned” with all of humanity.",
            "createdAt": "Tue Jul 28 22:06:56 +0000 2026",
            "viewCount": 169000,
            "metrics": {
              "views": 169000,
              "likes": 2266,
              "retweets": 219,
              "replies": 155,
              "quotes": 29,
              "bookmarks": 242
            },
            "media": [],
            "translation": {
              "provider": "google",
              "status": "success",
              "language": "zh-CN",
              "text": "马克·扎克伯格说得对：\n\n“我们这个时代的决定性问题不是超级智能是否存在，而是谁将能够获得它。它会集中并仅限于少数机构，还是会成为赋予每个人权力的工具？”\n\n权力集中是人工智能最大的风险。当少数实验室（与行政国家密切合作）决定谁有权使用哪种模型功能时，他们不可避免地会塑造可以说、知道和构建的内容。这不是“安全”。这是控制。\n\n正如马克指出的那样，开源的历史表明，广泛的访问和透明度通常是实现实际安全性和弹性的最佳途径。权力下放创造了权力制衡。相比之下，集中化的替代方案，如官僚审批制度和强制把关，通常会产生监管捕获并强化卡特尔。\n\n每个人手中的个人超级智能，具有竞争模型和真正的数据主权，比那些声称与全人类“结盟”的自封监护人更好地检查反乌托邦的未来。",
              "annotations": [],
              "error": "",
              "updatedAt": "2026-07-29T00:08:24.961Z",
              "version": 1
            }
